      "vocab_cloze_text": "In the early 1950s, African Americans in Southern states lived under the oppressive weight of [Jim Crow Laws], which legally enforced racial segregation in every sphere of public life. This system of legal inequality was anchored in the 1896 Supreme Court ruling [Plessy v. Ferguson], which had established the cynical doctrine of 'separate but equal'. To overturn this constitutional injustice, lawyers from the [NAACP] filed strategic legal lawsuits targeting school segregation in federal courts. Meanwhile, direct action advocates founded [CORE] to challenge segregated public facilities using non-violent picketing and sit-ins. Beyond segregated facilities, the most devastating instrument of white supremacy was political [Disenfranchisement], which barred over 95% of eligible Black adults from voting in Mississippi and Alabama through rigged literacy tests and poll taxes.",
