      "vocab_cloze_text": "On 17 May 1954, the legal landscape of the United States was revolutionized by the Supreme Court decision [Brown v. Board of Education], which declared that racial segregation in public schools violated the 14th Amendment. Newly appointed Chief Justice [Earl Warren] recognized the moral necessity of unity, working tirelessly behind closed doors to achieve an unequivocal 9-0 unanimous verdict. However, Southern states resisted with fierce obstinacy; in September 1957, Arkansas Governor [Orval Faubus] deployed armed state National Guardsmen to physically bar nine Black teenagers from entering Little Rock Central High School. Recognizing an unprecedented constitutional crisis where a state openly defied federal law, President Eisenhower dispatched 1,000 battle-hardened soldiers of the [101st Airborne Division] to escort the students past violent white mobs with fixed bayonets. Furious at his defeat, Faubus retaliated the following autumn by shutting down all four municipal high schools, inflicting [The Lost Year] upon thousands of local students before federal courts forced them to reopen.",
