      "vocab_cloze_text": "On 1 December 1955, respected seamstress and NAACP activist [Rosa Parks] was arrested in Montgomery, Alabama after quietly refusing to surrender her bus seat to a white passenger. That evening, community leaders rallied at Dexter Avenue Baptist Church and formed the [Montgomery Improvement Association] to coordinate an all-out boycott of municipal transit. The committee chose dynamic newcomer [Martin Luther King Jr.] as its president, captivated by his powerful message of Christian fellowship and disciplined non-violent resistance. Despite police intimidation, firebombings of leaders' homes, and arrests for hitchhiking, 40,000 Black citizens organized carpools and walked miles in the rain for [381 Days], depriving the city bus company of 70% of its operating revenue. The protracted standoff finally ended in triumph when the US Supreme Court affirmed [Browder v. Gayle], striking down city transit segregation and legally ending the boycott on 20 December 1956.",
