      "vocab_cloze_text": "Following the Brown ruling, Southern white leaders launched an aggressive campaign of 'Massive Resistance', formalised in 1956 when 101 senators and representatives signed [The Southern Manifesto], denouncing integration as an assault on state rights. In towns across the South, respectable bankers, landlords, and politicians flocked to [White Citizens' Councils], weaponising economic coercion by terminating mortgages, cancelling farm credit, and firing any Black resident who dared sign integration petitions. Alongside this economic pressure operated the violent terror of the [Ku Klux Klan], whose masked vigilantes detonated dynamite in Black churches, homes, and synagogues across Alabama and Mississippi. In Washington, powerful Southern senators known as [Dixiecrats] deployed marathon filibusters to paralyze the Senate and gut civil rights proposals. Although Congress ultimately enacted the [Civil Rights Act of 1957], Southern filibuster compromises stripped away its voter protection enforcement teeth, resulting in fewer than 3% more Black voters being registered over the following three years.",
