      "vocab_cloze_text": "On 1 February 1960, four Black college freshmen initiated the [Greensboro Sit-Ins] by sitting at a whites-only Woolworth's lunch counter in North Carolina and refusing to leave when refused service. Within weeks, the tactic swept through over fifty Southern cities, prompting student delegates to gather at Shaw University to establish [SNCC] as an independent frontline direct action organization. In May 1961, CORE director James Farmer launched the daring [Freedom Rides], sending mixed groups of Black and white riders on commercial buses into the Deep South to test Supreme Court desegregation decrees. The campaign met savage mob violence, most notably the horrific [Anniston Bus Bombing], where a Klan mob barricaded the doors of a burning Greyhound bus before riders barely escaped through broken windows. The struggle for higher education desegregation reached a bloody climax in 1962 when military veteran [James Meredith] attempted to register at Ole Miss, provoking campus riots that killed two people and forced President Kennedy to dispatch 30,000 soldiers and federal marshals to secure his enrollment.",
