      "vocab_cloze_text": "In spring 1963, Martin Luther King Jr. and the SCLC launched Project C in Alabama to confront notorious segregationist [Eugene 'Bull' Connor]. Imprisoned for defying an injunction against protesting, King composed the celebrated [Letter from Birmingham Jail] on scraps of newspaper, eloquently arguing that individuals had a moral duty to disobey unjust segregation statutes. When adult protest numbers dwindled due to fears of job losses, James Bevel organized the daring [Children's Crusade], sending hundreds of singing schoolchildren into the streets where Connor unleashed snarling police dogs and 100-pound water cannons on them. The harrowing televised footage horrified the American conscience, compelling local merchants to desegregate downtown stores and prompting the massive [March on Washington] in August 1963, where 250,000 demonstrators rallied for civil rights. However, white supremacist fury struck back weeks later with the heinous bombing of [16th Street Baptist Church], murdering four innocent young girls attending Sunday school and intensifying the urgent national demand for comprehensive federal reform.",
